# proxy-pools.conf — extra proxies for an SSID, one per row, separated by `|`.
# Copy to config/proxy-pools.conf. Do not add spaces around separators.
# Empty rows and rows beginning with `#` are ignored.
#
# This file is OPTIONAL. Without it — or for an idx with no row here — the SSID
# keeps using the single proxy from its wifi-socks.conf row, exactly as before.
# With rows here, a device is pinned to one of them and keeps it; the proxy in
# wifi-socks.conf stays as the default for devices not yet pinned.
#
# Columns:
#   idx        : Wi-Fi index from wifi-socks.conf. Rows for the same idx form
#                that SSID's pool.
#   proxy_type : socks5 or http.
#   host       : Proxy IP address or hostname.
#   port       : Proxy port, 1..65535.
#   user       : Proxy username; leave empty when authentication is disabled.
#   pass       : Proxy password; leave empty when authentication is disabled.
#   label      : OPTIONAL 7th column. Free text shown in the Console, at most
#                64 bytes. Purely cosmetic.
#
# The SLOT NUMBER is a row's position within its own idx, counted from zero.
# Slots decide TPROXY ports and are what /etc/sbproxy.assign pins devices to, so
# reordering or deleting a row remaps every device pinned after it. Add new
# proxies at the end of an idx's block whenever you can.
#
# Never commit the real proxy-pools.conf: it holds proxy passwords.
#
# idx|proxy_type|host|port|user|pass|label
1|socks5|1.2.3.4|1080|user1|pass1|VN-01
1|socks5|1.2.3.5|1080|user1|pass1|VN-02
1|http|5.6.7.8|8080|||US-01
3|socks5|proxy.example.com|1080|user3|pass3|SG-01
